# Normalise text files to LF in the repository, and check them out as LF on
# every platform.
#
# The `eol=lf` half is the part that matters here. `text=auto` alone stores LF
# but leaves checkout to core.autocrlf, which is `true` on a typical Windows
# install -- so the working tree would be rewritten to CRLF and the next commit
# touching any file would carry a whole-file diff. Pinning LF keeps what is
# committed and what is on disk the same bytes.
#
# Nothing here needs CRLF: PowerShell, MSVC's rc.exe and the Dart toolchain all
# read LF fine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one exception, kept for whoever adds a batch helper later: cmd.exe is the
# last thing on Windows that still genuinely wants CRLF, and mis-parses labels
# and `goto` without it.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Icons: the app icon compiled into the .exe via windows/runner/Runner.rc, the
# tray icon loaded as a Flutter asset, and the OpenMoji original it came from.
# `binary` marks them -text -diff, so they are never line-ending normalised.
*.ico binary

# third_party/local_notifier is upstream code carrying a one-function patch
# (see third_party/local_notifier/FORK.md). Marking it vendored keeps its Dart,
# C++, Swift and Objective-C out of GitHub's language statistics, so those
# describe this app rather than its dependency, and collapses it by default in
# pull request diffs.
third_party/** linguist-vendored
